          "text": "{\"evidence\":[{\"paragraph_id\":\"p1\",\"quote\":\"The first one corresponds to the sidereal rotation period (or sidereal day ), i.e., the time that the object takes to complete a full rotation around its axis relative to the background stars ( inertial space ). The other type of commonly used \\\"rotation period\\\" is the object's synodic rotation period (or solar day ), which may differ, by a fraction of a rotation or more than one rotation, to accommodate the portion of the object's orbital period around a star or another body during one day.\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p2\",\"quote\":\"For gaseous or fluid bodies, such as stars and giant planets , the period of rotation varies from the object's equator to its pole due to a phenomenon called differential rotation . Typically, the stated rotation period for a giant planet (such as Jupiter, Saturn, Uranus, Neptune) is its internal rotation period, as determined from the rotation of the planet's magnetic field .\"},{\"paragraph_id\":\"p37\",\"quote\":\"Jupiter 0.41354 days(average) 0.4135344 days (deep interior [ iv ] ) 0.41007 days (equatorial) 0.4136994 days (high latitude) 0 d 9 h 55 m 30 s [ 3 ] 0 d 9 h 55 m 29.37 s [ 3 ] 0 d 9 h 50 m 30 s [ 3 ] 0 d 9 h 55 m 43.63 s [ 3 ] 0.413 58 d (9 h 55 m 33 s) [ 10 ] (average)\"}]}"
